---
// Section — What SpecScore is.
// Two-column on desktop:
//   Left  = confident definition + hierarchy diagram, then a
//           hairline-ruled warm "why this matters" sub-section.
//   Right = tabbed artifact view (idea | feature | plan) with slide
//           animation. Feature tab default.
//
// Voice ordering follows `marketing/voice-and-tone.md` § Rule 1 — "Lead
// with the opinion, not the disclaimer." The position (what SpecScore
// is) comes first; the warm problem paragraphs serve as lived-experience
// evidence underneath, behind a small visual anchor so skimmers can find
// them.
---

<section class="what-is section" aria-labelledby="what-is-title">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2 id="what-is-title" class="what-is__title">
      A format for the specs you already write.
    </h2>
    <div class="what-is__grid">
      <div class="what-is__col what-is__col--text">
        <p class="what-is__intro">
          <strong>SpecScore is a Markdown + YAML format</strong> for features,
          requirements, and acceptance criteria. It defines a structure your
          team and your AI agents both understand — and it's just files in
          your repo.
        </p>
        <p class="what-is__intro">
          A SpecScore project is three artifact kinds along a lifecycle:
          an <strong>Idea</strong> captures a half-formed direction; a
          <strong>Feature</strong> spec freezes the agreed behavior; a
          <strong>Plan</strong> decomposes the feature into ordered tasks
          that each cite the acceptance criteria they satisfy.
        </p>

        <p class="what-is__intro">
          <strong>Why your agent cares.</strong> Because the structure is
          machine-readable, AI agents can query the spec through the CLI
          instead of reading every file. Ask <em>"what should I work on
          next?"</em> and the CLI returns open Features and unanswered
          questions. Same shape your team reviews; far less context the
          agent has to load.
        </p>

        <figure
          class="what-is__diagram"
          aria-label="SpecScore hierarchy. A Feature contains Requirements (REQ). Each Requirement is verified by Acceptance Criteria (AC), written as Given / When / Then."
        >
